such as thin-walled tubes or channels [7–10]. Moreover, L-PBF offers superior advantages over traditional methods for manufacturing complex structures like the Gyroid variant of triply periodic minimal surface (TPMS) [11]. It enables precise fabrication, crucial for aerospace, automotive, and medical applications, by directly translating digital designs into physical objects layer by layer. This approach enhances design flexibility, reduces material waste, and shortens manufacturing lead times, thus driving the optimization of structures across industries. L-PBF is regarded as a potential novel AM technology [7] and finds applications in various industries such as jewelery, automotive, oil refinery, marine, construction, aerospace, and aviation [12]. However, despite its numerous advantages, L-PBF faces challenges in achieving satisfactory surface quality, particularly in industrial applications, where surface roughness significantly impacts component performance. According to Boschetto et al. [13], surface roughness in L-PBF can vary within the range of 5 to 40 μm, which is lower than other metal-based AM techniques such as electron beam melting (25–130 μm) [14] and wire arc AM, where the surface roughness measurements often exceed several hundred micrometers [15]. While L-PBF has the capacity to achieve the best surface quality among metalbased AM, it frequently falls short of meeting industrial standards. Surface roughness is a common challenge in AM, particularly in L-PBF processes, and is widely recognized as a cause of premature failures [15, 16]. It is a critical quality determinant in L-PBF-produced parts, influencing various aspects such as tribological properties, fatigue performance, and overall mechanical integrity [5, 14, 17]. Studies investigating the impact of surface roughness on the fatigue life of Ti6Al4V parts produced by L-PBF highlight early fractures, poor mechanical properties, and compromised fatigue performance [18–21]. Currently, the surface roughness of AlSi10Mg alloy parts produced by L-PBF is a major concern in the aerospace and automotive industries, as it directly affects fatigue life and corrosion resistance [21]. Poor surface finish can lead to increased friction, wear, and susceptibility to crack initiation, ultimately affecting the lifespan and reliability of components. Studies on the relationship between arithmetic surface roughness (Ra) and wear resistance reveal a decrease in volumetric wear rate as the Ra value decreases [22]. The correlation between surface peaks (Rp), valleys (Rv), and corrosion resistance further underlines the interaction between surface conditions and material performance [22–24]. The impact of surface roughness on general corrosion, metastable pitting formation, and pitting potential adds layers of complexity to achieving the best surface quality, particularly given the interdependent nature of L-PBF process parameters [25–27]. Poor surface roughness has several detrimental effects, including reduced thermal
conductivity [28], increased risk of crack initiation [29], lower electrical conductivity [30], and decreased effectiveness of lubrication [31]. The impact of surface roughness on the performance of final products is illustrated in Fig. 1. To mitigate surface roughness issues, post-processing methods such as machining, shot peening, and chemical processing are employed. However, post-processing the inner surfaces of complex geometries remains challenging, and the associated costs often negate the advantages gained through AM [13, 27, 28]. The ideal of producing operational L-PBF parts without post-processing remains challenging, especially for applications such as aeronautics [32]. The prominent techniques were employed to study the influential process parameters with respect to the desired quality characteristics like categorizing the source of parameters and following the cause-and-effect analysis. In L-PBF, the sources of process parameters can be categorized into laser parameters, powder characteristics, feedstock material properties, and printing chamber environment [33]. Finding the responsible process parameters is crucial. Cause-andeffect analysis emerges as a valuable tool in this quest [34]. L-PBF emerges as a promising approach due to its ability to produce complex geometries with minimal waste [35], but challenges such as parameter dependency and material characteristics exist. AlSi10Mg, a widely used cast aluminum alloy in L-PBF, is in high demand across high-tech industries due to its exceptional strength-toweight ratio and favorable casting properties. Despite these advantages, the mechanical characteristics and surface roughness of AlSi10Mg present challenges, which are further complicated due to the high thermal conductivity
