and reflectivity of aluminum [29, 30]. Currently, several research and development efforts aim to overcome these challenges and achieve stringent quality standards for AlSi10Mg printed parts. The complex interaction of factors including material properties, thermal conductivity, and diverse process variables creates barriers to attaining consistent quality and reliability in the final components. To address these challenges, machine learning introduces novel opportunities to model interactions and predict responses more accurately. For example, a study analyzed the effects of laser power, scanning speed, and hatch distance on surface roughness (Ra) using multilayer perceptron (MLP) and adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference system (ANFIS), with MLP demonstrating superior predictive capability [36]. Another investigation examined similar parameters, including layer thickness, utilizing an artificial neural network (ANN) with backpropagation, achiev
ing an impressive R2 of 99%, indicating high accuracy [37]. Linear regression, support vector machines, random forests, gradient boosting algorithms, and artificial neural networks are among the traditional machine learning algorithms commonly employed for predicting surface roughness in additively manufactured components [38]. According to Xu et al. [39], the effectiveness of machine learning models relies not only on the volume and accuracy of the data but also significantly on the choice of the modeling algorithm. Certain algorithms are particularly suited for modeling with limited data. The algorithms well suited for handling small datasets encompass support vector machines, Gaussian process regression, random forest, gradient boosting decision trees, eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), and symbolic regression [39]. The L-PBF printing process involves numerous process parameters, each affecting the quality of the component in different ways. Considering all these parameters together to achieve specific quality characteristics is not efficient. It is crucial, therefore, to focus on key process parameters that most significantly influence the desired quality outcomes. Previous studies have explored various L-PBF effects of process parameters, such as laser power, scan speed, hatch spacing, and layer thickness, which significantly influence surface roughness [40–42]. However, the specific parameters considered in different studies vary, indicating that the literature on the effects of L-PBF process parameters on surface roughness is not yet mature. In this study, we explored the interactions between L-PBF process parameters and surface roughness utilizing machine learning models to uncover underlying patterns and dependencies within the process. Particularly, random forest regression (RFR) and support vector regression (SVR) were considered based on the nature of the available dataset, quantity (small dataset), and capacity of the models to predict the surface roughness.
The study primarily focuses on AlSi10Mg, which was selected due to its significance in high-tech industries. The motivation for the study is the recognition of the critical importance of enhancing surface quality in L-PBF, as poor surface quality often leads to various premature failures. Moreover, it has been noted that the process parameters considered across different studies to analyze their effects on surface quality vary, highlighting the need for further investigation to systematically identify crucial parameters. Therefore, this study aims to contribute to the existing body of knowledge by formulating cause-and-effect mappings to pinpoint key process parameters responsible for surface roughness. It addresses the effects of these parameters on surface roughness and predicts their impacts using machine learning models, particularly RFR and SVR, followed by a comparison of their results to draw conclusions.
2 Materials andmethods
2.1 AlSi10Mg powder
AlSi10Mg, an aluminum alloy containing aluminum, silicon, and magnesium, is a popular material for 3D printing due to its favorable mechanical properties, lightweight nature, and corrosion resistance. In this study, gas-atomized AlSi10Mg powder from m4p material solutions GmbH was used. The particle size distribution (PSD) of the powder was identified as D10 (23.41 μm), D50 (45.52 μm), and D90 (61.45 μm). Figure 2 shows (a) the particle size distribution and (b) a scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of the powder.
2.2 Parameter selection
To focus on the most significant factors and reduce the number of involved variables in the experiment, the relationship among the identified process parameters was thoroughly analyzed. As illustrated in Fig. 3, the cause-and-effect approach, guided by the “5Why?” principle, was implemented to identify the responsible process parameters for surface roughness formation. Accordingly, the detailed cause-and-effect analysis indicated that the laser power, scanning speed, layer thickness, and part orientation are the crucial parameters influencing surface roughness. Other parameters were held constant to streamline the experimentation process. Acknowledging the necessity of considering a full range of factors that may impact surface quality for a thorough understanding, the cause-and-effect analysis was derived mainly from literature and experience to limit the problem’s complexity. The comparison presented in Table 1 put together the process parameters selected for sample fabrication in this study with those considered by other researchers in the


