2.4 Design ofexperiment
In this study, the design of experiment (DoE) according to the Taguchi method was employed to systematically examine the impacts of L-PBF process parameters on surface roughness. The Taguchi mixed design L16, outlined in Table 2, was chosen for its efficiency in analyzing multiple factors and their interactions while minimizing the number of experimental runs required [51–53]. To ensure reproducibility, triplicate samples were produced for each test case. The samples were then fabricated according to the DoE with four levels of laser power (P) and scanning speeds and two levels of layer thickness (Lt) and part orientations (θ). As detailed in Sect. 2.1, all other parameters were maintained constant during sample production. Specifically, a bidirectional scanning strategy was utilized for its fast production capability, with a tilt angle of 67° and a hatching distance of 100 μm. Statistical methods commonly used for surface roughness assessment include the metrics arithmetical mean (Ra) and root-mean-square (Rq) roughness parameters, which depict the vertical variances in surface height relative to a designated reference plane [50]. In this study, the roughness parameters according to ISO 4287, namely, the amplitude parameters (Rv and Rp) and the amplitude average parameters (Ra and Rq), were considered to characterize the surface roughness of AlSi10Mg prints.
