2.5 Developing machine learning model
Machine learning, a subclass of artificial intelligence (AI), encompasses various techniques that enable computers to learn from data and make predictions or decisions. These methods include the following:
(i) Supervised learning, where models predict outcomes from labeled data (ii) Unsupervised learning, which identifies patterns in unlabelled data (iii) Reinforcement learning, where agents learn decisionmaking through rewards or penalties
The challenge of ML needs large data to make accurate predictions of complex relationships such as natural language processing, image recognition, and autonomous systems [54]. However, certain algorithms are well suited for modeling with limited data, including support vector machines, Gaussian process regression, random forest, gradient boosting decision trees, eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), and symbolic regression [39]. In this study, the relationship between L-PBF process parameters and surface roughness was explored using machine learning models to uncover patterns and dependencies. Given the small dataset and the objectives, RFR and SVR were utilized, and their results were compared.
2.5.1 Data preparation
The data preparation process in these models included the data saved in a CSV (comma-separated values) file extracted. The variables representing features (laser power, scanning speed, layer thickness, and part orientation) and targets (Ra and Rq) were identified. To ensure consistency, feature scaling was performed by using StandardScaler from the Sklearn. preprocessing module. For predicting surface roughness, two machine learning algorithms (RFR and SVR) were employed. The dataset was divided into training (70%) and test (30%) sets for both models.
