3 Results anddiscussions
3.1 Experimental results
The experimental results are summarized in Table 2. The results depicted in Fig. 7 illustrate the influence of key process parameters, as identified through cause-and-effect analysis, on surface roughness. The variations in laser power and layer thickness, particularly when combined with higher scanning speeds, consistently correlate with alterations in surface roughness across the examined power range. For instance, the outcomes observed in two extreme cases, A1 and B8, are illustrated in Fig. 7a and b, respectively, showing the effects of parameters on surface patterns. In Fig. 7a, lower laser power and scanning speed contribute to a relatively smoother surface finish, particularly noticeable at lower layer thicknesses. However, the observation of unfused powder particles on the surface indicates incomplete fusion, potentially attributed to variations in PSD and their differential response to thermal effects. This suggests that while lower power and speed may improve surface finish, they can also lead to issues with incomplete material fusion.
On the other hand, Fig. 7b demonstrates that higher laser power levels lead to increased powder melting, which results in greater surface roughness due to the rapid formation of the melt pool. The significant influence of layer thickness is evident in terms of achieving proper fusion with the previous layer. This indicates that while higher power ensures better melting, it may poorly affect surface smoothness due to the aggressive thermal input. These findings emphasize the interaction between process parameters and the resulting surface quality in L-PBF processes. The observed trends highlight the importance of parameter optimization, as balancing these parameters is crucial to achieving the desired surface characteristics. This discussion emphasizes the necessity of process control in L-PBF to mitigate surface roughness while ensuring complete material fusion. Main effect plots are utilized to show the contribution of individual process parameters, assessing their significance in causing variations in surface roughness. These analyses aid in determining which parameters employ the greatest influence on surface roughness and exposing how alterations in these parameters impact the quality of printed parts. The impacts of each level of process parameters on surface roughness are depicted in Fig. 8, where a smaller is better signal-to-noise ratio analysis was conducted. The main effect plot shows low values for Ra and Rq results (Fig. 8a and b) implying improved surface roughness. However, better results for Rv (Fig. 8c) were observed at high laser power, medium scanning speed, low layer thickness, and inclined build angle. Similarly, better Rp results (Fig. 8d) were obtained at low laser power, high scanning speed, low layer thickness, and inclined part orientation. The improved results for Rq were achieved at low laser power, scanning speed, thickness, and flat build angle. The results in Table 3 offer additional insights into the degree of influence contributed by each selected process parameter, with consideration given to both Ra and Rq. Layer thickness proves most influential, closely followed by laser power, with scanning speed and build angle ranking third and fourth, respectively.
