3.3 Surface characteristics
During the L-PBF process, various surface characteristics and defects significantly influenced the quality of printed parts, including spattering (fine particle ejection) and balling (undesirable clump formation), which impact the quality of printed layers. Spattering occurs due to high-energy laser interactions, leading to the expulsion of molten material [55]. Additionally, porosity, characterized by voids within the material, may result from inadequate powder fusion or gas entrapment [56]. Blistering, characterized by bubble-like structures, is often linked to gas entrapment during melting. Many of these common defects manifest on the surface, thereby influencing surface roughness parameters, as depicted in Fig. 10. Balling and spattering are observed during the L-PBF process, particularly under conditions of elevated laser power. Low laser power levels contribute to incomplete melting and poor fusion of powder particles, resulting in the formation of unconsolidated clusters and irregularities on the surface, as evidenced by increased Rp (peak height) and Rv (valley depth) values. Moreover, specific conditions
