for Ra, indicating better performance, while R2 = 66% for Rq, which is moderate. This difference in model performance is visually agreed by the plotted data points in Fig. 12, which align closely with the metrics findings. Particularly, the plot reveals a systematic trend in Rq predictions, with most data points falling below the regression line, suggestive of a tendency for the model to underestimate Rq values. This trend
corresponds well with the lower R2 value observed for Rq,
indicating a less comprehensive capture of variance compared to Ra. In the case of Ra, the plot depicts relatively a tighter alignment between predicted and actual values,
reflecting a higher R2 value and implying a more accurate interpretation of the underlying data relationships. This combined analysis indicates the significance of both numerical and visual assessments to evaluate the developed model.
3.4.3 Comparison themodels
In this study, the effects of the L-PBF process parameters on surface roughness were predicted by the two models, namely, RFR and SVR. After careful hyperparameter optimization, the models’ performance was assessed using the
R2, MAE, and RMSE, and the results are given in Table 4. The RFR model outperformed the SVR model in predicting both Ra and Rq. This indicates that the RFR model was more effective in capturing data variance and delivering more accurate predictions. Similarly, for Ra, the RFR model exhibited superior performance, significantly higher than the SVR model, and showed lower error metrics, reflecting more reliable and precise predictions. The analysis of the graphical plots (Figs. 11 and 12) confirmed that the RFR model outperformed the SVR model. The RFR model showed strong alignment between predicted and actual values for both Ra and Rq, indicating high accuracy and low error rates. The SVR model tended to predict lower Rq values, and while it performed better for Ra, it still did not match the accuracy of the RFR model. Overall, the RFR model proved to be more robust and reliable for predicting surface roughness in this context.
4 Conclusions
This research investigates the effect of L-PBF process parameters on surface roughness through employing machine learning techniques. L-PBF, a prominent metalbased AM technique, offers manufacturing flexibility and complex design possibilities. However, some challenges related to surface roughness in L-PBF-printed parts, particularly for applications in high-tech industries, remain a critical concern. This study is aimed to contribute to the existing knowledge on the identification of the critical
