parameters that influence the surface quality of parts fabricated by L-PBF. Based on this study, the following conclusions are drawn.
The cause-and-effect analysis results were proven, confirming that the identified factors (build angle, scanning speed, and layer thickness) indeed have a significant impact on the final product’s surface finish. Layer thickness proves most influential, closely followed by laser power, with scanning speed and build angle ranking third and fourth, respectively. The findings demonstrate that higher LED values correlate with increased amplitude average parameters (Ra and Rq), indicating rougher surfaces. Insufficient LED can lead to poor adhesion and rough surfaces, while excessive energy causes excessive melting. Higher VED tends to stabilize and decrease Ra and Rq, indicating improved material consolidation and reduced overall roughness. The correlation observed between amplitude parameters (Rv and Rp) and energy density input (LED and VED) suggests the presence of additional influencing factors, thereby necessitating further investigation. The machine learning models, specifically RFR and SVR were employed to predict the effects of these process parameters on surface roughness. The RFR model
achieved R2 values of 97% and 85% for predicting Ra and Rq, respectively, while the SVR model achieved 85% and 66%, respectively. Evaluation metrics demonstrated that the RFR model outperformed the SVR model in predicting surface roughness, which implies that FRF model is more effective in capturing data variance and delivering better prediction with the dataset.
